\details{
The Uniform distribution has probability density function:
\deqn{f(x) = \frac{1}{b - a}, \quad a \le x \le b}
and zero elsewhere, where \eqn{a} is \code{min} and \eqn{b} is \code{max}.
For \code{nv_dunif}, \code{nv_punif}, and \code{nv_qunif}, the \code{min} and \code{max} are
converted to the data type of \code{x}/\code{q}/\code{p}.

All four are univariate functions evaluated elementwise, returning one
value per element of \code{x}/\code{q}/\code{p} (or of the sample). Non-scalar \code{min}/\code{max} therefore give a
separate univariate Uniform per element, \emph{not} a multivariate Uniform over
the hyper-rectangle \eqn{\prod_i [a_i, b_i]}. For that, reduce over the
result: \code{nv_prod(nv_dunif(x, min, max))}, or
\code{nv_sum(nv_dunif(x, min, max, log = TRUE))} on the log scale.
}
\section{Random generation}{

\code{nv_runif} samples from the open interval \eqn{(a, b)}.

\code{min} and \code{max} are \code{\link{arrayish}}, so they may vary across the sample: they
are applied to the draws after they have been reshaped to \code{shape}, and so
may either be scalars or have exactly that shape. As in base R's \code{runif()},
an element whose \code{min} equals its \code{max} is that value, and one whose \code{min} or
\code{max} is not finite, or whose \code{max} is less than its \code{min}, is \code{NaN}. The RNG
state is advanced regardless.
